>To confuse matters further, apparently the Authorised Version of the 
>bible used (allegedly 100% faithfully) the already archaic strict plural-
>singular distinction. As the most familiar example of this is the Lord's 
>prayer, in which God the Father is addressed using "thy", some confusion 
>has resulted. The AV itself does not restrict use of "thee" to God - Jesus 
>addresses Peter as "thee" for example.

Actually, my understanding is that the AV used the formal ("you") /
familiar ("thou") distinction of its time (early 1600s). Not only in
the Lord's Prayer, but throughout the translation, God is addressed
using the familiar form, emphasizing the Reformation view of an
immanent, personal God, in contrast to the Medieval conception which
emphasized God's transcendence.

Unfortunately, this pronoun form was left unchanged throughout six
revisions of the AV (the last being in 1769), while "thou" and "thee"
were falling out of use. The result was a mistaken impression that the
archaic terms were terms of respect--exactly the opposite of what the
translators were trying to convey.

